slug: effect-sizes-confidence-intervals-p-values
title: Effect Sizes, Confidence Intervals and P-Values — What They Do and Do Not Mean
area: Research & Evidence
level: Clinical
best: Second year onwards
minutes: 14
hook: "p < 0.05" does not mean the treatment is important, and "p > 0.05" does not mean it does not work. Many clinicians misread the most quoted number in science. Learn what the p-value really says, and why the effect size and its confidence interval tell you far more. || "p < 0.05" ka matlab yeh nahi ke ilaaj ahem hai, aur "p > 0.05" ka matlab yeh nahi ke kaam nahi karta. Bohat se clinicians science ke sab se zyada quote kiye jane wale number ko ghalat parhte hain. Seekhein ke p-value asal mein kya kehta hai, aur effect size aur uska confidence interval aap ko kahin zyada kyun batate hain.
goal: By the end, you'll define a p-value in one sentence, interpret an effect size and a 95 percent confidence interval, compare them with the minimal important change, and avoid the common traps such as "no significant difference means no difference". || End tak aap p-value ki ek jumle mein tareef karenge, effect size aur 95 fisad confidence interval ki tafseer karenge, unhein minimal important change se milayenge, aur aam jaalon se bachenge jaise "significant farq nahi matlab farq nahi".
say: An **effect size** is the size of the difference between groups (for example, 8 points on a 100-point pain scale). A **confidence interval (CI)** is the range of values that plausibly contains the true effect, given the data; a 95 percent CI is calculated so that, if the study were repeated many times, about 95 percent of such intervals would contain the true value. A **p-value** is the probability of getting a result at least this extreme **if there were truly no effect** (and the assumptions hold). It is **not** the probability that the treatment works. || **Effect size** groups ke beech farq ka size hai (masalan 100 point ke dard scale par 8 point). **Confidence interval (CI)** un values ki range hai jis mein data ke mutabiq asal asar hone ka imkaan hai; 95 fisad CI is tarah nikalta hai ke agar study baar baar dohrayi jaye to takreeban 95 fisad aise intervals mein asal value hogi. **P-value** woh imkaan hai ke is jitna ya zyada intehai nateeja aaye **agar waqai koi asar na ho** (aur assumptions theek hon). Yeh ilaaj ke kaam karne ka imkaan **nahi** hai.
analogy: Think of **a smoke alarm**. A p-value is like asking, "If there is no fire, how often would the alarm ring like this by chance?" A low p-value says "unlikely by chance alone", but it does not tell you how big the fire is or whether the building is in danger. The effect size is the size of the fire; the confidence interval is how sure you are about that size. || **Smoke alarm** sochein. P-value aisa hai jaise poochhna, "Agar aag nahi hai to alarm ittifaq se is tarah kitni baar bajega?" Kam p-value kehta hai "sirf ittifaq se na-mumkin", lekin yeh nahi batata aag kitni bari hai ya imarat khatre mein hai ya nahi. Effect size aag ka size hai; confidence interval is size ke baare mein aap ka yaqeen hai.
compare: Reading results: p-value, effect size and confidence interval || Nateeje parhna: p-value, effect size aur confidence interval
head: Number || Number ;; What it tells you || Yeh kya batata hai ;; What it does NOT tell you || Yeh kya nahi batata
row: P-value || P-value ;; How surprising the data would be if there were no effect || Agar koi asar na ho to data kitna hairat angez hota ;; How big the effect is, how important it is, or the chance the treatment works || Asar kitna bara hai, kitna ahem hai, ya ilaaj ke kaam karne ka imkaan
row: Effect size || Effect size ;; The size of the difference, in units you can understand || Farq ka size, aise units mein jo aap samajh saken ;; Whether it is precise or whether it matters to patients || Kya yeh durust hai ya mareezon ke liye ahem
row: 95 percent confidence interval || 95 fisad confidence interval ;; The range of plausible true effects, showing precision || Mumkina asal asaron ki range, durusti dikhati hui ;; A 95 percent chance that the true value lies inside this one interval (a common misreading) || Is ek interval mein asal value hone ka 95 fisad imkaan (aam ghalat fehmi)
row: Minimal important change || Minimal important change ;; The smallest change that matters to patients (your yardstick) || Sab se chhoti tabdeeli jo mareezon ke liye ahem hai (aap ka paimana) ;; Not part of the statistics; you compare the interval with it || Shumariyat ka hissa nahi; aap interval ko is se milate hain
check: A trial finds that a treatment reduces pain by 4 points (95 percent CI 1 to 7) on a 100-point scale, p = 0.01. Patients say they need 15 points to feel a difference. What is the best reading? || Trial paata hai ke ilaaj 100 point scale par dard 4 point kam karta hai (95 fisad CI 1 se 7), p = 0.01. Mareez kehte hain farq mehsoos karne ke liye 15 point chahiye. Behtareen tabsira kya hai?
ok: Statistically significant but too small to matter, since even the top of the interval (7) is below 15 || Shumariyati taur par significant lekin ahem hone ke liye bohat chhota, kyunke interval ka oopar wala hissa (7) bhi 15 se kam hai || Yes — significance is not importance. || Haan — significance ahmiyat nahi.
no: Clearly effective, since p is below 0.05 || Saaf mu'assir, kyunke p 0.05 se kam hai || It ignores the size of the effect. || Yeh asar ke size ko nazar-andaaz karta hai.
no: Ineffective, since the CI includes 1 || Be-asar, kyunke CI mein 1 shamil hai || The CI does not include zero here. || Yahan CI mein zero shamil nahi.
check: A small trial finds a difference of 12 points (95 percent CI -3 to 27), p = 0.12. The authors write "the treatment has no effect". What is wrong with that? || Chhote trial mein 12 point ka farq milta hai (95 fisad CI -3 se 27), p = 0.12. Musannif likhte hain "ilaaj ka koi asar nahi". Is mein kya ghalat hai?
ok: The interval includes both no effect and a large benefit; the study is inconclusive, and "no significant difference" is not "no difference" || Interval mein asar na hona aur bara faida dono shamil hain; study ghair-faislakun hai, aur "significant farq nahi" ka matlab "farq nahi" nahi || Yes — absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. || Haan — saboot ka na hona ghair-maujoodgi ka saboot nahi.
no: Nothing, because p is above 0.05 || Kuch nahi, kyunke p 0.05 se oopar hai || The p-value cannot prove no effect. || P-value asar na hona sabit nahi kar sakta.
no: The p-value should have been below 0.01 || P-value 0.01 se kam hona chahiye tha || The problem is interpretation, not the cut-off. || Masla tafseer ka hai, had ka nahi.
check: Which statement correctly describes a p-value of 0.03? || P-value 0.03 ko kaun sa bayan sahi bayan karta hai?
ok: If there were truly no effect, data this extreme would occur about 3 percent of the time || Agar waqai koi asar na hota to is jitna intehai data takreeban 3 fisad waqt aata || Yes — it is a statement about data under no effect. || Haan — yeh asar na hone ki surat mein data ke baare mein bayan hai.
no: There is a 97 percent chance the treatment works || Ilaaj ke kaam karne ka 97 fisad imkaan hai || That reverses the logic. || Yeh mantiq ulta karta hai.
no: The effect is 3 percent || Asar 3 fisad hai || The p-value is not an effect size. || P-value effect size nahi.
mistake: **Using p = 0.05 as a cliff edge.** A result with p = 0.049 and p = 0.051 are practically the same, yet one is called "significant" and the other "not". Do not treat the line as a switch between "works" and "does not work". Look at the effect size, the interval and whether the whole interval lies on the useful side of the minimal important change. Also, remember that very large studies can make tiny, unimportant effects "significant". || **P = 0.05 ko dhalwan ki tarah istemal karna.** p = 0.049 aur p = 0.051 amali taur par ek jaise hain, phir bhi ek ko "significant" aur doosre ko "nahi" kaha jata hai. Is lakeer ko "kaam karta hai" aur "nahi karta" ka switch na samjhein. Effect size, interval aur is baat ko dekhein ke kya poora interval minimal important change ki mufeed taraf hai. Yeh bhi yaad rakhein ke bohat bade studies chhote, ghair ahem asaron ko bhi "significant" bana sakte hain.
clinic: **Translate numbers into plain words.** For example: "On average, people who did the programme had about 8 points less pain than those who did not. The true effect could plausibly be anywhere from 3 to 13, so it is likely to help, but how much will vary." This respects the patient's intelligence and keeps expectations honest. || **Numbers ko saade alfaaz mein badlein.** Misaal: "Average mein, programme karne walon ko na karne walon se takreeban 8 point kam dard tha. Asal asar 3 se 13 ke beech kahin bhi ho sakta hai, isliye madad ka imkaan hai, lekin kitni madad hogi woh mukhtalif hogi." Yeh mareez ki aqal ki izzat karta hai aur umeedein imaandar rakhta hai.
reveal: Why do some journals now discourage the phrase "statistically significant"? || Kuch journals ab "statistically significant" lafz kyun pasand nahi karte? || Because "significant" is widely misread as "important" or "true", and because a bright line at 0.05 encourages cherry-picking and p-hacking. The American Statistical Association warned that a p-value does not measure the probability that a hypothesis is true or the size or importance of an effect, and that scientific conclusions should not rest only on whether p passes a threshold. Reporting effect sizes with intervals is the recommended alternative. || Kyunke "significant" ko aam taur par "ahem" ya "sach" samjha jata hai, aur 0.05 par roshan lakeer chun-chun kar lene aur p-hacking ko barhawa deti hai. American Statistical Association ne khabardar kiya ke p-value kisi hypothesis ke sach hone ka imkaan ya asar ka size ya ahmiyat nahi naapta, aur science ke nateeje sirf is par na hon ke p had se guzra ya nahi. Effect sizes ko intervals ke saath report karna sifarish shuda mutabadil hai.
case: Two trials of the same exercise for knee pain are reported. Trial A (400 patients) finds a 3-point difference, p = 0.001. Trial B (30 patients) finds a 10-point difference, p = 0.09, 95 percent CI -2 to 22. || Trial A is statistically very clear but the effect may be tiny; Trial B is imprecise, with a wide interval that includes a large benefit. || She compares both with the 10-point minimal important change: A is precise but too small, B is promising but unclear. || She tells the patient: "The exercise probably helps a bit, perhaps more for some people; it is low risk, so it is worth trying."
safety: **Never dismiss a possible harm because p is above 0.05.** A small study that finds more adverse events with a treatment, with a wide interval, has not shown the treatment is safe. Look at the harm estimate and its interval, and err on the side of caution when the interval includes serious harm. || **P 0.05 se oopar hone par mumkina nuqsan ko radd na karein.** Chhota study jo ilaaj ke saath zyada adverse events paaye, wasi interval ke saath, usne yeh sabit nahi kiya ke ilaaj mehfooz hai. Nuqsan ka andaaza aur uska interval dekhein, aur jab interval mein seriyas nuqsan shamil ho to ehtiyat ki taraf jhukein.
evidence: The American Statistical Association's statement on p-values set out six principles, including that p-values can indicate how incompatible the data are with a specified statistical model, that they do not measure the probability that the studied hypothesis is true, and that a p-value alone does not measure the size of an effect or the importance of a result. Sullivan and Feinn argued that reporting effect sizes alongside p-values is essential, since statistical significance depends strongly on sample size and says nothing about practical importance. || American Statistical Association ke p-values par statement ne chhe usool tay kiye, jin mein yeh ke p-values batate hain ke data tay shuda shumariyati model se kitna na-mutabiq hai, yeh kisi hypothesis ke sach hone ka imkaan nahi naapte, aur akela p-value asar ka size ya nateeje ki ahmiyat nahi naapta. Sullivan aur Feinn ne daleel di ke p-values ke saath effect sizes report karna lazmi hai, kyunke shumariyati significance sample size par bohat munhasir hai aur amali ahmiyat ke baare mein kuch nahi kehti.
memory: **Size, Spread, Surprise.** Look at the size of the effect, the spread of the interval, and only then the surprise of the p-value. || **Size, Phailao, Hairat.** Pehle asar ka size, phir interval ka phailao, aur sab se aakhir mein p-value ki hairat dekhein.
summary: Effect size says how big, the confidence interval says how precise, and the p-value says how surprising the data would be if there were no effect. || Effect size batata hai kitna bara, confidence interval kitna durust, aur p-value batata hai agar asar na hota to data kitna hairat angez hota.
summary: Compare the interval with the minimal important change; significance is not importance, and non-significance is not no effect. || Interval ko minimal important change se milayein; significance ahmiyat nahi, aur non-significance ka matlab asar na hona nahi.
summary: A p-value above 0.05 never proves safety; check harm estimates and their intervals. || 0.05 se oopar p-value safety kabhi sabit nahi karta; nuqsan ke andaaze aur intervals dekhein.
card: What does a p-value tell you? || P-value kya batata hai? || How surprising the data would be if there were no true effect. || Agar asal asar na ho to data kitna hairat angez hota.
card: What does a confidence interval show? || Confidence interval kya dikhata hai? || The range of plausible true effects, showing precision. || Mumkina asal asaron ki range, durusti dikhate hue.
card: Does "not significant" mean no effect? || Kya "not significant" ka matlab asar nahi? || No; the study may be too small to tell. || Nahi; study itni chhoti ho sakti hai ke pata na chale.
card: Can a tiny effect be "significant"? || Kya chhota asar "significant" ho sakta hai? || Yes, in a very large study. || Haan, bohat bade study mein.
deeper: Critical appraisal in ten minutes || /learn/critical-appraisal-in-ten-minutes || Reading intervals against the smallest change that matters. || Intervals ko sab se chhoti ahem tabdeeli se milakar parhna.
deeper: Outcome measures and minimal important change || /learn/outcome-measures-minimal-important-change || The yardstick for importance. || Ahmiyat ka paimana.
source: Wasserstein RL, Lazar NA. The ASA statement on p-values: context, process, and purpose. Am Stat 2016;70:129-133. || The ASA statement on p-values context process and purpose Wasserstein Lazar
source: Sullivan GM, Feinn R. Using effect size, or why the P value is not enough. J Grad Med Educ 2012;4:279-282. || Using effect size or why the P value is not enough Sullivan Feinn
